WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984
A local LLM server is a stack.

2
00:00:01.984 --> 00:00:07.440
Hardware provides CPU, GPU, VRAM, system memory, disk, and network capacity.

3
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.722
A runtime loads the model file, prepares prompts, manages context, and generates tokens.

4
00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:20.005
An API layer receives requests from chat clients, coding assistants, scripts, or agents.

5
00:00:20.005 --> 00:00:27.280
Load balancing works only when each layer is understood well enough to predict what a backend can handle.

6
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.512
The model file shapes capacity.

7
00:00:29.512 --> 00:00:38.026
Size, format, quantization level, context capability, and runtime compatibility affect memory use and response speed.

8
00:00:38.026 --> 00:00:46.210
A smaller quantized model may fit on modest hardware, while a larger model may require more VRAM and longer load times.

9
00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:54.890
A long context setting can help with document analysis, but it can also increase prompt processing time and memory pressure.

10
00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:08.282
Most clients interact with local servers through HTTP APIs, often using concepts similar to cloud LLM APIs: model names, chat-style request bodies, streaming chunks, and structured errors.

11
00:01:08.282 --> 00:01:11.506
Compatibility is helpful but not guaranteed.

12
00:01:11.506 --> 00:01:18.450
Endpoint paths, request fields, headers, authentication behavior, and error formats can differ.

13
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.831
A load balancer must preserve the behavior the client expects.

14
00:01:22.831 --> 00:01:25.394
Streaming is especially important.

15
00:01:25.394 --> 00:01:36.471
Many interfaces expect partial output while the model is still generating, so the backend, proxy, and load balancer must avoid buffering the entire response.

16
00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:40.356
Each backend should also be monitored as its own system.

17
00:01:40.356 --> 00:01:50.607
A server with a different model, smaller context window, older runtime, or weaker GPU may need different routing rules instead of equal treatment.
